press_for: 90d }\n \n sidecars:\n@@ -471,7 +476,7 @@ overrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n \n Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n \n-**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` is written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. Until #344 and #345 land, `ard init` behaves as before: PR only, conflicts unless `--force`, label sync first.\n+**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a