nt scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. Until #344 and #345 land, `ard init` behaves as before: PR only, conflicts unless `--force`, label sync first.\n \n **`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config. Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\n \n@@ -488,7 +493,7 @@ Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit\n - **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bod